\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Write $\mathbb D=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|<1\}$ and $\mathbb T=\partial\mathbb D$. The class $S$ consists of holomorphic injective functions $f:\mathbb D\to\mathbb C$ normalized by $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$, so $f(z)=z+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}a_nz^n$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is it true for every $f\in S$ that \[\limsup_{n\to\infty}\bigl||a_{n+1}|-|a_n|\bigr|\le1?\] \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}
Update 6.73 reports Hamilton's stronger eventual bound $\bigl||a_{n+1}|-|a_n|\bigr|\le1$. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Are successive coefficient magnitudes of every normalized univalent function eventually separated by at most one in the limiting sense?

\subsection{Sources}
